    ("Fe\u2013Cr\u2013Ni was screened at T = 1000 K, below liquid appearance and at a service-relevant temperature, for a lean-nickel austenitic window: fully austenitic (FCC \u2265 0.99), sigma-free and melt-free (each \u2264 10\u207b\u00b3), with nickel capped at 12 mol% (Table 14, Figure 12). The screen evaluated 501,501 compositions on a 10\u207b\u00b3 simplex grid in 0.4 s, shortlisting 10,936 hits (2.18%). The whole shortlist was re-solved with the full 28-phase set \u2014 all 11 Pareto-frontier points (minimal Ni per Cr bin, falling from 7.5% Ni at 1.5% Cr to 4.8% near 12\u201313% Cr), a random 400-point subset, and the remaining 10,525 in a follow-up run, with no solver failures \u2014 so shortlist precision is a census and not an estimate: 10,936 of 10,936 confirm. Recall is bounded instead, because the screen's false negatives were sampled rather than enumerated. A uniform draw of the non-shortlisted in-scope points found 4 qualifying alloys among 76, a miss rate of 5.3% with a Clopper\u2013Pearson 95% interval of 1.5\u201312.9%. Extrapolated uniformly over the 102,925 in-scope non-hits this implies about 5,400 missed qualifying alloys and a recall of 0.67 (95% interval 0.45\u20130.88). The estimate is a worst case: all four observed misses lie at Cr \u2264 0.20, and the miss rate is very likely lower in the chromium-rich part of the window. Three of the four sit within 0.01 of a decision cutoff and one is a clear model miss, and ensemble spread separates them from the validated hits (Figure 11, panel a; AUROC 0.86, n = 4, so suggestive rather than conclusive; the clear failure carries ten times the shortlist 95th-percentile spread). The threshold sweep over the 811-point boundary-enriched validation pool gives precision 1.00 at all nine FCC\u2013sigma combinations (Figure 11, panel b; Table 14); because 411 of those 811 points are shortlist members already counted above, this corroborates robustness to the cutoff choice rather than re-estimating deployed precision."),
